1
00:00:30,120 --> 00:00:41,420
Thank you for watching. Please subscribe to my channel.

2
00:00:41,880 --> 00:00:51,420
Welcome everybody. My name is Rachel and this is my friend Nia and she is somewhere where it is cold.

3
00:00:51,510 --> 00:01:12,510
But she's going to be ambitious and she's going to go to the gym after our conversation. And I think you might understand why after our conversation. So Nia, we were talking about looking back over this past year, looking forward and looking at making goals and taking care of ourselves. So would you like to share?

4
00:01:12,570 --> 00:01:16,010
what you came up with because I know you put a lot of thought into this.

5
00:01:16,710 --> 00:01:31,750
Sure. As a person living with dementia, I find that even though there are things that I can no longer do, it's important, more important to focus on what I can do. And that doesn't mean that I can't have goals for myself.

6
00:01:31,950 --> 00:01:58,190
And so for the new year, I went ahead and came up with some new years, not really resolutions, but goals to where I can engage my mind, spirit, and body. And they're done by the week to make them. So they're short term goals, but they're things that can help people like me living with dementia to live well and live better lives.

7
00:01:58,980 --> 00:02:01,780
I like that idea of putting it by the week.

8
00:02:02,490 --> 00:02:15,870
Yes, by the week. For instance, when it comes to the mind, these are some of my things that I'm doing. I'm completing a one crossword puzzle a week.

9
00:02:16,980 --> 00:02:36,320
So that is my goal because sometimes you know we find that we may sit and watch a little bit too much TV but for those of us living with dementia we really need to engage our minds. So that is one of my goals is as and also journaling my thoughts.

10
00:02:46,980 --> 00:02:57,220
and use punctuation.

11
00:02:57,360 --> 00:03:27,340
While your mind is still fresh and good. And the next one is embracing gratitude and possibly just saying thank you more every day. Whether it just be, you know, when I wake up in the morning, the first thing that I say is thank you, Lord, because he woke me up. So embracing a spirit of gratitude. And along those lines, also for every negative thing, think a positive or proclaim something positive.

12
00:03:27,360 --> 00:03:53,460
And that will also help our minds to think more, get those endorphins going and just think more, get the dopamine going and just engage those other cells in our minds. And the last one for your mind that I'm doing is I'm revisiting a hobby, or I may even try a new one. I used to be able to sketch.

13
00:03:53,550 --> 00:04:01,790
Rachel and I used to be able to draw anything that I could see. Wow. So I'm going to try and sketch.

14
00:04:02,670 --> 00:04:14,410
Again, so those are a few things that I'm doing to engage my mind in the new year. And my goal is to sketch something or engage in a new hobby every week.

15
00:04:14,550 --> 00:04:18,450
And some things that you may be able to do is to check out

16
00:04:18,630 --> 00:04:27,570
If you have an adult community college, sometimes they have like cooking classes or learning instrument classes.

17
00:04:27,720 --> 00:04:33,060
These are things that people living with dementia can do. We can still do these things.

18
00:04:33,240 --> 00:04:33,900
.

19
00:04:33,960 --> 00:04:48,100
As Rachel, as you said, I'm going to the gym when I get out of here and one of my goals, we have to, if it's good for the heart, it's good for the mind. So my goal is to exercise three to four times a week.

20
00:04:48,420 --> 00:04:49,380
Hi.

21
00:04:49,410 --> 00:04:52,350
And to also go for walks.

22
00:04:52,530 --> 00:04:54,910
And drink eight.

23
00:04:55,560 --> 00:05:03,180
8 ounces of water a day along with make an effort to eat more of the Mediterranean diet.

24
00:05:03,720 --> 00:05:13,880
Because the omegas and the olive oils and the fish and the legumes, the fruits and vegetables, if it's good for the heart, it's good for the mind.

25
00:05:14,370 --> 00:05:22,410
So I have that I'm going to every day I'm going to eat two vegetables, two fruits, one fish and use olive oil.

26
00:05:23,640 --> 00:05:50,900
So that's my everyday challenge. Wow, Nia! Yes. And lastly, for the spirit, which is so important because our spirits are what keeps us going. It is what we exude into the world, into the universe. And so one thing that I'm going to do is I made a day, which is my Sundays, to call loved ones.

27
00:05:51,570 --> 00:06:04,210
Even though I talk to people throughout the week, I made a point to call and speak to loved ones because having that social connection as a person living with dementia is so important.

28
00:06:04,350 --> 00:06:12,310
And I take time out of the day to not watch so much TV, but at least an hour of just listening to music.

29
00:06:13,290 --> 00:06:27,930
So I'm using music therapy also and that's a goal of mine one hour a day at least of listening to music to get those other little sparks in my brain going and to calm me down whenever I'm feeling anxious.

30
00:06:28,320 --> 00:06:35,020
And last but not least, if it's good for the spirit, it's good for the community, volunteerism.

31
00:06:35,280 --> 00:06:41,740
Because we can still do things. We can still volunteer and be active.

32
00:06:41,880 --> 00:06:55,540
members of the community, whether it be at a food bank, whether you're volunteering at a library or at your church. These are all good things that we can still do as people living with dementia.

33
00:06:56,820 --> 00:07:06,720
And that's what I have for my New Year's resolutions. Wow, Nia, I'm really impressed. And something I noticed, kind of a

34
00:07:07,440 --> 00:07:17,540
A key thing in that was how specific you were. And I know people talked about you setting goals and the idea of making it specific.

35
00:07:17,610 --> 00:07:22,970
So it's, you know, I'm going to listen to music one hour a day. I'm going to do a crossword puzzle.

36
00:07:23,040 --> 00:07:36,300
I'm going to have two fruits and two vegetables and one fish and cook with all of what. So you, I like how you've made it so concrete. So it makes it easier to know whether you actually

37
00:07:36,330 --> 00:07:37,110
Done it!

38
00:07:37,140 --> 00:07:39,500
Or, "Well, I got close."

39
00:07:39,690 --> 00:07:48,570
Not today. Yes, they're called SMART goals. Specific, measurable,

40
00:07:48,840 --> 00:07:51,160
attainable, achievable,

41
00:07:51,690 --> 00:07:54,030
Realistic and time.

42
00:07:54,660 --> 00:08:04,800
you know time achieved or something like that. So yeah, they're smart goals. I learned that many years ago that you have to be deliberate.

43
00:08:05,100 --> 00:08:11,760
You know, because if you're aiming for nothing, you're not going to hit anything, you know.

44
00:08:11,880 --> 00:08:16,940
Yeah, because you're not trying to go anywhere specific, so it's just kind of aimless.

45
00:08:17,130 --> 00:08:21,830
So you have set out, I'm trying to go here and I'm going to do it by

46
00:08:21,930 --> 00:08:26,490
These different steps and I'm going to do it this often.

47
00:08:26,580 --> 00:08:28,440
And this is why.

48
00:08:29,070 --> 00:08:30,450
Yes.

49
00:08:30,810 --> 00:08:33,590
Well, I'm really excited for you.

50
00:08:33,900 --> 00:08:58,100
Thank you. I, you know, there's so many things that come with being a person living with dementia and many times people focus on the end result, which is, you know, the last stages of dementia. But prior to that, we can still live fruitful lives. We still can, but we have to take hold of our quality of life.

51
00:08:58,470 --> 00:09:01,730
as well and not just succumb to the disease.

52
00:09:01,950 --> 00:09:08,750
Well, I've heard you say, isn't it something about you're fighting to do the best you can so that you're doing

53
00:09:09,030 --> 00:09:13,930
You're not just giving up, you're kind of giving dementia a run for its money and you're making it.

54
00:09:13,980 --> 00:09:26,620
as hard as you can for it to take things just on your own. You're making it work for it. You're exercising, you're paying attention to what you're eating, you're doing things that are good for your brain and your body. You're

55
00:09:27,180 --> 00:09:47,820
Being and interacting with people, you're making a difference in people's lives. And you're right, a lot of people seem to miss that middle part. Everyone focuses on the end. And some, I'm not sure what happens to the middle part, but looks like you're really taking that middle part and saying, and I'm going to do the best I can with what I have while I have it.

56
00:09:47,880 --> 00:09:49,420
And I admire you for that.

57
00:09:50,010 --> 00:09:53,970
Thank you, Rachel. I couldn't do it without people like you.

58
00:09:56,410 --> 00:10:00,010
No, it's support and it's community and it's people working together.

59
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:00,460
All right.

60
00:10:00,990 --> 00:10:01,690
Yes.

61
00:10:02,520 --> 00:10:08,360
Well, thank you for sharing your goals with us and how you have taken that time. I know you've

62
00:10:08,460 --> 00:10:21,660
You've actually taken time and pulled back to really think and look over the past year and look at where you were and where you wanted to be and how you're going to get there. And thank you for sharing that with us.

63
00:10:22,230 --> 00:10:25,430
Thank you, Rachel. I appreciate you having me.

64
00:10:25,650 --> 00:10:26,450
Thank you.

65
00:10:27,390 --> 00:10:35,830
Hi, I'm Tipa Snow and you just found our YouTube channel and watched one of our videos. I'm the owner and founder of Positive Approach to Care.

66
00:10:35,880 --> 00:10:36,380
*laughs*

67
00:10:36,600 --> 00:10:39,820
Thanks for watching and if you liked,

68
00:10:39,990 --> 00:10:45,070
If you have a comment about or you would, please share it with people you know.

69
00:10:45,090 --> 00:10:48,030
And if you haven't yet done it, consider subscribing.

70
00:10:48,180 --> 00:10:52,740
We'll let you know when the next new video comes out. And you might want to visit our website.

71
